jects • Position the patient on the MRI table • Acquire images in different planes and sequences • Use contrast material only when indicated **Indications** • Ligaments ACL PCL collateral ligament and selected shoulder injuries • Tendons and muscles Rotator cuff or Achilles tears muscle tears hematomas and tendinopathy • Joints Meniscal tears cartilage injury effusion synovitis and osteonecrosis • Spine Disc herniation spinal stenosis nerve root compression spinal cord abnormalities and infection • Tumors Extent of bone marrow and surrounding soft tissue involvement **Possible findings** • Ligament or tendon tears • Muscle injury and cartilage damage • Bone marrow edema • Disc herniation or nerve compression • Soft tissue masses **Advantages** • No ionizing radiation • Excellent soft tissue contrast • Shows bone marrow abnormalities • Useful for ligaments tendons cartilage and spinal structures • Images can be obtained in multiple planes **Limitations** • Expensive and often takes longer than X ray or CT • Claustrophobia may be a problem • Some implants or metallic foreign bodies need special safety assessment • Movement can reduce image quality • Some abnormalities also occur in people without symptoms **Physiotherapy importance** Supports assessment of soft tissue and spinal conditions helps plan rehabilitation precautions and may guide referral MRI findings must be matched with symptoms and examination **Conclusion** MRI is valuable for soft tissue bone marrow and spinal disorders and does not use ionizing radiation","thinking":"The user wants me to reformat this medical text according to their strict formatting rules.","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:30:37Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nI removed these lines because I copied them from the pdf : BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes    and all page x -      so start your work : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------        Investigations for Musculoskeletal Disorders BPT 3rd Year Physiotherapy | Easy-language university exam notes | 10-mark answers Reference: David J. Magee, Orthopedic Physical Assessment (assessment principles). Simplified study notes; not verbatim textbook content. Contents • 1. Blood Investigations and Markers • 2. Ultrasonography (USG) • 3. X-ray (Radiography) • 4. Computed Tomography (CT Scan) • 5. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) Page 1 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes1. Blood Investigations and Markers Definition: Blood investigations are laboratory tests performed on a blood sample to detect inflammation, infection, autoimmune disease, muscle injury, and metabolic problems affecting bones and joints. In simple words: Blood tests help identify whether a musculoskeletal problem may be due to infection, inflammation, an immune disorder, or a metabolic problem. Aims • Detect infection and inflammation. • Identify autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis. • Assess bone and mineral metabolism. • Detect muscle damage. • Support diagnosis and differential diagnosis. • Monitor disease activity and response to treatment. Important blood investigations • CBC (Complete Blood Count): Measures red cells, white cells, hemoglobin, and platelets. Increased WBCs may suggest infection; low hemoglobin may indicate anemia; platelets may rise during inflammation. • ESR (Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate): Measures how far red blood cells settle in one hour. It may rise in inflammatory disorders and infections. It is a nonspecific marker and cannot identify the exact disease. • CRP (C-Reactive Protein): A protein produced mainly by the liver during inflammation. It rises in infection and inflammatory disorders and is useful for monitoring treatment \n<truncated 8042 bytes>\n to a strong magnetic field. • Radiofrequency pulses change their alignment temporarily. • As they return, they produce signals. • A computer converts these signals into detailed images. Procedure • Screen the patient for MRI safety, including implants and metallic objects. • Position the patient on the MRI table. • Acquire images in different planes and sequences. • Use contrast material only when indicated. Indications • Ligaments: ACL, PCL, collateral ligament, and selected shoulder injuries. • Tendons and muscles: Rotator cuff or Achilles tears, muscle tears, hematomas, and tendinopathy. • Joints: Meniscal tears, cartilage injury, effusion, synovitis, and osteonecrosis. • Spine: Disc herniation, spinal stenosis, nerve-root compression, spinal cord abnormalities, and infection. • Tumors: Extent of bone marrow and surrounding soft-tissue involvement. Possible findings • Ligament or tendon tears. • Muscle injury and cartilage damage. • Bone marrow edema. • Disc herniation or nerve compression. • Soft-tissue masses. Advantages • No ionizing radiation. • Excellent soft-tissue contrast. • Shows bone marrow abnormalities. • Useful for ligaments, tendons, cartilage, and spinal structures. • Images can be obtained in multiple planes. Limitations • Expensive and often takes longer than X-ray or CT. • Claustrophobia may be a problem. • Some implants or metallic foreign bodies need special safety assessment. Page 8 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes• Movement can reduce image quality. • Some abnormalities also occur in people without symptoms. Physiotherapy importance: Supports assessment of soft-tissue and spinal conditions, helps plan rehabilitation precautions, and may guide referral. MRI findings must be matched with symptoms and examination. Conclusion: MRI is valuable for soft-tissue, bone marrow, and spinal disorders and does not use ionizing radiation.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T20:00:37+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
