{"step_index":12,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T17:38:27Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nAssessment - Demographic Data. Chief complaint, History of Present illness including functional limitation, Past Medical Surgical history, Family history, Occupational & Personal history, Socio-Economic, Environmental and Psychological History, Drug and Treatment History etc. Musculoskeletal Disorders:Vital parameters (Temperature, Respiratory Rate, Peripheral Pulses, Blood Pressure). Body Built of Patient, Artitude of Limb, Bony contours, Deformities, Trophic changes.\r\nExamination of Posture. Examination of Spasm, Tenderness, Edema /swelling/wasting including Limb girth measurement. Scar examination. Motor Examination: Range of Motion, Joint Play & End Feel, Muscle Strength, Power, Endurance, Flexibility, Isometric Resisted Testing, Limb-length discrepancy, Pelvic inclination, Examination of Balance, Gait & External aids/assistive Device used etc. Sensory Examination: Superficial & Deep sensation, Reflexes, Dermatomes, and Myotomes etc.((Dermatomes\r\nDefinition: Areas of skin supplied by sensory fibers from a single spinal nerve root.\r\nPurpose: Helps identify sensory loss due to nerve root lesions.\r\nExamples:\r\nC6: Thumb.\r\nC7: Middle finger.\r\nC8: Little finger.\r\nL4: Medial side of leg.\r\nL5: Dorsum of foot and great toe.\r\nS1: Lateral side of foot.\r\nNormal: Sensation is intact in the tested dermatome.\r\n5. Myotomes\r\nDefinition: Groups of muscles predominantly supplied by a single spinal nerve root.\r\nPurpose: Assesses motor function of spinal nerve roots.\r\nExamples:\r\nC5: Shoulder abduction.\r\nC6: Wrist extension.\r\nC7: Elbow extension.\r\nC8: Finger flexion.\r\nL2: Hip flexion.\r\nL3: Knee extension.\r\nL4: Ankle dorsiflexion.\r\nL5: Great toe extension.\r\nS1: Ankle plantarflexion.\r\nNormal: Full strength, usually 5/5 on MMT.)) ((Assessment of Hand Function\r\n1. Hand Pinches\r\nDefinition: Holding an object between the thumb and fingers.\r\nTypes: Tip-to-tip, lateral pinch, three-jaw chuck pinch.\r\nAssessment: Pinch gauge.\r\nExample: Holding a key or picking up a coin.\r\n2. Hand Grip\r\nDefinition: Ability to grasp and hold ob\n<truncated 1550 bytes>\n in young adults but can indicate heart failure.\r\nS4: May occur with reduced ventricular compliance.\r\nNormal breath sounds:\r\nVesicular: Soft, low-pitched sounds over most lung fields.\r\nBronchial: Heard normally over the trachea.\r\nBronchovesicular: Heard near the major bronchi.\r\nAbnormal breath sounds:\r\nCrackles: Popping sounds, e.g., pulmonary edema or pneumonia.\r\nWheeze: Musical sound, e.g., asthma.\r\nRhonchi: Low-pitched sounds due to airway secretions.\r\nStridor: Harsh sound due to upper airway obstruction.\r\nReduced or absent breath sounds: May occur with pneumothorax or pleural effusion.\r\n2. Chest Expansion\r\nDefinition: Measurement of the increase in chest circumference during inspiration.\r\nMethod: Measure chest circumference during maximal expiration and inspiration using a measuring tape.\r\nCommon sites: Axillary, nipple line and xiphisternal level.\r\nNormal: Approximately 3–5 cm in healthy adults, depending on age and measurement site.\r\nExample: Reduced chest expansion in COPD.\r\n3. Symmetry of Chest Movement\r\nDefinition: Assessment of equal movement of both sides of the chest during breathing.\r\nMethod: Observe chest movement or place hands symmetrically over the chest.\r\nNormal: Both sides expand equally during inspiration.\r\nAbnormal: Unequal chest expansion.\r\nExample: Reduced movement on one side in pleural effusion or pneumothorax.\r\n4. Body Mass Index (BMI)\r\nDefinition: Measurement of body weight in relation to height.\r\nFormula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m²).\r\nNormal adult BMI (WHO): 18.5–24.9 kg/m².\r\nExample: Weight 60 kg, height 1.65 m, BMI = 22.0 kg/m².\r\n5. Waist–Hip Ratio (WHR)\r\nDefinition: Ratio of waist circumference to hip circumference.\r\nFormula: WHR = Waist circumference ÷ Hip circumference.\r\nNormal reference values (WHO):\r\nMen: ≤0.90\r\nWomen: ≤0.85\r\nExample: Waist 80 cm, hip 100 cm, WHR = 0.80.\r\nPurpose: Assesses abdominal fat distribution and associated health risk.))\r\n\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T23:08:27+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
