{"step_index":18,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:03:48Z","content":"--- **1 Neck Disability Index NDI** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Howard Vernon and Silvano Mior in 1991 as a modification of the Oswestry Low Back Pain Disability Questionnaire • It is a patient reported condition specific outcome measure designed to quantify self rated disability and functional limitation in patients experiencing acute subacute or chronic neck pain including cervical spondylosis radiculopathy and whiplash associated disorders • **Structure and Clinical Domains** • Comprises 10 itemized sections evaluating physical daily activities and symptom impact • Pain Intensity severity of baseline cervical aching/pain • Personal Care washing dressing grooming without exacerbating symptoms • Lifting handling light medium or heavy objects • Reading sustained cervical flexion/postural tolerance • Headaches frequency and duration of cervicogenic headaches • Concentration ability to focus on cognitive tasks • Work ability to perform occupational duties • Driving head turning blind spot checks postural endurance • Sleeping sleep disruption due to neck symptoms • Recreation participation in hobbies sports and leisure • **Scoring Methodology and Formula** • Each of the 10 sections is rated on a 6 point ordinal scale ranging from 0 no limitation/no pain to 5 complete limitation/worst pain • Maximum possible raw score equals 50 when all 10 sections are completed • If all 10 sections are answered • Percentage Disability equals Total Raw Score / 50 x 100 • Handling missing sections eg patient does not drive • Minimum completed sections required for validity 9 out of 10 • Percentage Disability equals Total Raw Score / 5 x Number of completed sections x 100 • **Score Interpretation** • 0 to 4 points 0 percent to 8 percent No disability • 5 to 14 points 10 percent to 28 percent Mild functional disability • 15 to 24 points 30 percent to 48 percent Moderate functional disability • 25 to 34 points 50 percent to 68 percent Severe functional disability \n<truncated 7105 bytes>\n9 Social Life • Section 10 Travelling • **Scoring Methodology and Calculation** • Each section contains 6 statements scored from 0 to 5 • 0 indicates the least limitation or no pain • 5 indicates the greatest functional restriction or maximum pain • Maximum possible raw score equals 50 if all 10 sections are completed • Calculation when all sections are answered • ODI Percentage percent equals Total Raw Score / 50 x 100 • Calculation when Section 8 Sex Life is omitted • ODI Percentage percent equals Total Raw Score / 45 x 100 • **Score Interpretation and Clinical Grading** • 0 percent to 20 percent Minimal Disability The patient can cope with most living activities Treatment usually centers on ergonomics core stabilization and general fitness • 21 percent to 40 percent Moderate Disability The patient experiences pain with sitting lifting and standing Conservative management manual therapy electrotherapy active directional preference exercises is indicated • 41 percent to 60 percent Severe Disability Pain remains the main problem activities of daily living are significantly disrupted Requires detailed diagnostic evaluation and structured intervention • 61 percent to 80 percent Crippled Back pain impinges on all aspects of the patients life Intervention requires advanced pain management multidisciplinary care or surgical consultation • 81 percent to 100 percent Bedbound or Symptom Exaggeration These patients are either completely bedridden or exaggerating their symptoms requires screening for non organic signs/Waddell signs • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Establishes functional baseline and tracks progress over weeks of lumbar core strengthening McKenzie extension or motor control stabilization • Serves as standard legal and disability documentation for return to work evaluations and medicolegal reporting • **Clinical Limitations** • Patients from conservative cultures frequently leave the Sex Life section blank necessitating manual formula adjustments • Shows","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining my tool selection process based on explicit rules. • I'm strictly adhering to the \"NEVER\" list for common bash commands, especially avoiding `cat` and `grep` within broader commands unless they are required. • The new critical instruction 2 demands I explicitly list related tools before execution and justifies the selection of tools. • This methodical approach aims to optimize tool usage and ensure a focused and efficient problem-solving workflow.\n\n\n**Structuring Data Outputs**\n\nI'm now focused on structuring outputs according to strict formatting rules • I must ensure a single continuous text block, with bolded main and subtopics • I'm using `---` to divide main topics and keeping numbers inline • Periods are replaced with bullets to ensure correct sentence separation in output • There is no new formatting on this turn.\n\nHere's the summary:\n\nI am working on structuring the output in a single block of text with bolded main and subtopics • I'm using `---` as a main topic divider and ensuring the inline numbers are preserved • Bullet points replace periods and other sentence separators like commas and semicolons.\n\n\n**Formatting Output Precisely**\n\nI'm now implementing strict formatting rules for the new content to ensure clarity and consistency. Each index, such as the Neck Disability Index, will be introduced with a `---` separator, along with its number. Subtopics will b