{"step_index":23,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:12:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-17T23:42:17+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-17T23:42:20+05:30\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n<truncated 1 lines>\nTransradial Route: Patient may ambulate within 1 to 2 hours; avoid wrist flexion, weight-bearing through the hand, and lifting objects over 2 to 3 kg for 48 to 72 hours.\r\nInspect the puncture site for active bleeding, expanding swelling/hematoma, or distal pulse loss (dorsalis pedis/radial artery) before initiating mobilization.\r\n7. Chest X-Ray (CXR)\r\nDefinition\r\nA chest radiograph is a non-invasive projection transmission image displaying the differential radiodensity of thoracic tissues: air (black/radiolucent), fat (dark gray), water/soft tissue (light gray), and bone/metal (white/radiopaque).\r\nStandard Projections\r\nPosteroanterior (PA) View: Gold standard; taken in full upright inspiration with X-ray beam travelling from back to front; minimizes cardiac magnification.\r\nAnteroposterior (AP) View: Performed at bedside on immobilized or ICU patients; magnifies cardiac silhouette size and limits scapular clearance.\r\nSystematic Evaluation Approach (ABCDE Method)\r\nA - Airway: Trachea position (normally midline, vertically oriented over spinous processes).\r\nB - Breathing / Lung Fields: Vascular markings, lucency, symmetric expansion, pleural spaces.\r\nC - Cardiac Silhouette & Mediastinum: Cardiothoracic Ratio (CTR) on PA view is normally less than 0.50 (50%); mediastinum shape and borders.\r\nD - Diaphragm: Both hemidiaphragms rounded, smooth, right sitting 1 to 2 cm higher than left; costophrenic and cardiophrenic angles sharp and clear.\r\nE - Everything Else / Skeleton: Ribs, clavicles, humeri, subcutaneous tissues, indwelling tubes, catheters, pacing wires.\r\nCommon Pathological Variations on CXR\r\nLobular Consolidation (Pneumonia):\r\nHomogeneous radio-opacity with preservation of lung volume.\r\nPresence of \"Air Bronchograms\" (branching lucent tubular airways outlined by fluid-filled alveoli).\r\nSilhouette sign: Loss of the right heart border indicates right middle lobe consolidation.\r\n<truncated 4230 bytes>\ncalf swelling greater than 3 cm compared to asymptomatic side (+1), pitting edema confined to symptomatic leg (+1), collateral superficial non-varicose veins (+1), alternative diagnosis as likely as DVT (-2).\r\nScore greater than or equal to 2: DVT likely (urgent Duplex Ultrasound required).\r\nHoman�s Sign:\r\nPassive dorsiflexion of the ankle with the knee extended to elicit calf pain.\r\nClinical Note: Insensitive, non-specific, and carries a mechanical risk of dislodging an unstable clot. It is no longer recommended in modern clinical guidelines.\r\nTrendelenburg Test (Brodie-Trendelenburg Test):\r\nDifferentiates saphenofemoral junction incompetence from deep/perforator valvular incompetence in varicose veins.\r\nLeg is elevated to empty superficial veins; a tourniquet is applied around the upper thigh.\r\nPatient stands: rapid retrograde filling from above before releasing the tourniquet indicates perforator incompetence; rapid filling from above after tourniquet release confirms saphenofemoral junction valve incompetence.\r\nVenous Duplex Ultrasound:\r\nGold standard non-invasive diagnostic investigation for DVT; evaluates venous compressibility and venous flow patterns.\r\nPhysiotherapy Implications and Precautions\r\nIn Intermittent Claudication (PAD with ABI 0.5 to 0.9): Supervised treadmill walking is the primary conservative therapy. Exercise to moderate-to-strong claudication pain (grade 3 on 4-point scale), rest until pain resolves, and repeat for 30 to 45 minutes, 3 to 5 times weekly.\r\nIn Severe PAD (ABI less than 0.5): High-intensity exercise, leg elevation, and external compression bandages are strictly contraindicated.\r\nIn Acute Confirmed DVT: Hold all ambulation, range of motion exercises, and calf massage until adequate therapeutic anticoagulation is established (typically 24 to 48 hours with Low Molecular Weight Heparin).\r\nIn Chronic Venous Insufficiency: Promote leg elevation above heart level, prescribe graduated compression stockings (20 to 30 mmHg), and prescribe active calf muscle pump exercises./////////\r\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
